## Outcome and conditions

Given spot and two futures maturities, compute the course basis and calendar spread, identify the supplied lecture orderings, and explain convergence using both spot/futures delivery directions with execution assumptions. Distinguish short selling an asset from a short futures position, EDSP procedure from a universal formula, and endpoint convergence from smooth movement. No cost-of-carry pricing model is included.

Finished-note locations: [[01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/Week_1/Basis_Convergence_and_Futures_Hedging.md#Basis and calendar spread are different comparisons]]; [[01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Financial_Markets_Instruments_I/Week_1/Basis_Convergence_and_Futures_Hedging.md#Why the prices converge at delivery]].

Supported learning permits these note sections and the worked key. Independent checks permit blank paper and a non-programmable calculator only; no notes, formula list, key, hints, external help or generated solution. The tutor must confirm these conditions and known prior item exposure before marking independence. Unknown conditions are unscorable for independence, even if the content is correct. These stricter practice conditions do not purport to reproduce all exam rules. Hypothetical tasks below instantiate only note-supported mechanisms.

## Prerequisites and checks

O006-P1 / v1 (O001): Does a June futures contract equal a September one, and does a short futures trade require borrowing the asset? Key different maturities are different obligations; short futures is selling the contract, distinct from borrowing an asset for a spot short sale. Pass both distinctions; partial one omitted; fail equivalence/borrowing asserted automatically. Repair O001 **How the obligation ends** reasoning in explanation and worked offset; then this lesson W1. Needed to compare prices of matching eligible deliveries and avoid treating futures/asset shorts alike.

A qualifying committed prerequisite result may skip its diagnostic only when its exact current tuple or explicitly approved compatible tuple and independence conditions are known. Familiarity is insufficient. Diagnostics do not count as acquisition checks. Do each relevant diagnostic once; failed/partial responses follow the stated repair, then one fresh full check rather than repeated diagnosis.

