## Rubric and independence

The required components are: basis_and_spread_conventions; ordering_and_drivers; two_arbitrage_directions_and_limits; edsp_and_endpoint. Each full check below explicitly elicits and keys every component. Pass requires all its essential reasoning and requested outputs without a critical error. Partial means a correct mechanism but a missing component, unresolved sign/unit, or isolated arithmetic slip. Fail means a critical conceptual error persists or the method cannot establish the result. Unscorable means the response/conditions cannot be interpreted reliably; ask the existing prompt once for clarification, without adding a new assessment. Accept algebraically equivalent expressions, clearly defined opposite cash-flow signs, and plain-language explanations with the same mechanism. An arithmetic slip is partial until independently corrected; it is not automatically a conceptual failure. No numerical score is inferred.

Critical errors: Basis/spread confused; signs assigned without convention; either arbitrage direction reversed; unrestricted/guaranteed arbitrage claimed without costs/feasibility; EDSP universal formula or convergence monotonic forecast.

Acquisition requires two distinct unassisted passes from the explicitly permitted routes O006-I1/O006-I2, O006-I1/O006-D1, O006-I1/O006-D2, O006-I2/O006-D1, O006-I2/O006-D2, O006-D1/O006-D2. I items normally establish acquisition; an unused D item may replace an exposed or failed I item and then cannot later be counted as delayed retention. Each selected full item assesses all listed components, so every route covers them twice. W1, S1, prerequisite diagnostics and the narrow C1 correction are forbidden substitutes. The D bank is finite: never repeat an exposed item to certify fresh learning and never invent changed numbers. If fewer than two unused full items remain, raise a plan_issue for a new prepared bank.

