### O006-I1

**Prompt:** At a time before delivery S100,F^X105,F^Y110, X earlier. Compute B^X and D, state sign conventions and lecture ordering; name the Week 1 drivers without constructing a pricing model. At delivery compare F120/S110 and F110/S120: give executable legs and gross profit for each, explain borrowing an asset versus short futures and the assumptions. State convergence, EDSP's role and whether it predicts monotonic paths.

**Key and essential rubric:** B=S−F^X=−5,D=F^X−F^Y=−5; normal contango110>105>100. Drivers expectations/carrying-opportunity costs/expected payments. Delivery first spot buy110/short-deliver120 profit10; reverse long-delivery110/sell spot120 profit10 (borrow/return if spot short before delivery). Short futures merely opens contract; not the asset-borrowing operation. Eligible matching asset, feasible trades/delivery/borrowing and ignored costs essential. F_T=S_T,B_T0 conditional. EDSP contract-defined final reference, no universal formula; no smooth/monotonic prediction. All four components required.

### O006-I2

**Prompt:** Before delivery S100,F^X96,F^Y92. Compute basis/spread under course conventions, classify lecture ordering and name introduced price-gap drivers. At delivery analyze F55/S50 and F50/S55, including legs/gross differences, asset short-sale distinction and limits. What do EDSP and convergence establish, and what do they not establish about interim paths?

**Key and essential rubric:** B+4,D+4; normal backwardation92<96<100. Expectations/carry and opportunity cost/expected payments. F55/S50: buy eligible spot50/deliver short55, gross5. F50/S55: long delivery50/spot sale55,gross5; borrowing-return if needed for earlier spot short, distinct from futures short. Require execution/matching and ignored costs; otherwise simple arbitrage conclusion unsupported. Conditional F_T=S_T,B_T0; EDSP final reference procedure not universal average formula; no monotonic interim movement. All four components required.

### O006-D1

**Prompt:** S70,F^X74,F^Y77 before delivery. Compute and interpret course B/D and normal ordering; name supported drivers only. At delivery use F90/S80 then F80/S90 to derive both leg pairs and gross profits. Distinguish borrowed-asset short from short futures and state feasibility/cost assumptions, EDSP role and convergence/path limits.

**Key and essential rubric:** B−4,D−3, normal contango77>74>70. Introduced drivers expectations/carry-opportunity costs/expected payments, no later pricing model. First spot buy80/short delivery90,10; reverse long delivery80/sell spot90,10. Borrowing/return belongs to asset short sale if needed, futures short opens obligation. Eligible matching/feasible trade/delivery and ignored costs support F_T=S_T,B_T0. EDSP defined final reference not universal formula. Endpoint equality does not assert smooth/monotonic paths. All four components required.

### O006-D2

**Prompt:** S200,F^X190,F^Y180, X earlier. Calculate B/D with conventions, lecture ordering and note-supported drivers. At delivery compare F210/S200 and F200/S210: specify legs/gross difference each, short-selling versus short-futures distinction, assumptions, EDSP/convergence claim and whether declining futures throughout is necessary.

**Key and essential rubric:** B+10,D+10, normal backwardation180<190<200. Drivers expectations/carry-opportunity costs/payments. First buy spot200/short deliver210 profit10; reverse long receive200/spot sell210 profit10; asset borrowing/return only for borrowed spot short. Execution, eligibility, matching and ignored costs required. F_T=S_T,B_T0 under assumptions; EDSP final specified procedure, not universal rule. No monotonicity required. All four components required.

